exercises.explanation:
Copy on the way in, and build new orders instead of changing one:

```java
Order { lines = List.copyOf(lines); }

Order withLine(String line) {
    var next = new ArrayList<>(lines);
    next.add(line);
    return new Order(id, next);
}
```

The tests catch the plausible half-measures. Wrapping with `Collections.unmodifiableList(lines)` instead of copying passes the "cannot add through the accessor" test but fails "changing the source list later does not change the order", because the wrapper is a view of the caller's list. Storing the list as given fails both. A `withLine` that calls `lines.add` on the stored list either throws (on a copy) or changes the original order, and the wither test checks the original still has one line.

