exercises.explanation:
Compare the double the way the record does and hash the boxed value:

```java
@Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) return true;
    if (!(o instanceof ReadingClass other)) return false;
    return Objects.equals(sensor, other.sensor) && Double.compare(value, other.value) == 0;
}
@Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(sensor, value); }
```

The tests compare the class with the record on the cases where `==` and `Double.compare` disagree. Writing `value == other.value`, the natural first attempt, fails both: `NaN` readings come out unequal (a `HashSet` keeps every one of them, the set test sees 3 instead of 2) and `0.0` equals `-0.0` while the record says not. Leaving `hashCode` as `Object`'s identity hash breaks the same set test even with a correct `equals`, because two equal `NaN` readings land in different buckets, and the hash test checks that equal readings hash alike. A missing `null` check throws from `equals(null)` instead of returning false.

